{
  "_createdAt": "2026-04-27T21:40:59Z",
  "_id": "wp-post-102748-en",
  "_rev": "dJ4ZpMyfIFtQ0D7lyxhY4t",
  "_type": "article",
  "_updatedAt": "2026-07-05T16:12:09Z",
  "author": {
    "_ref": "author-legacy-archive",
    "_type": "reference"
  },
  "body": [
    {
      "_key": "000000000001",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000000",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Egypt's historical places, seen as a list, are too many."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000003",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "fjrz3v4ck1mj",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Pyramids, Sphinx, "
        },
        {
          "_key": "umc2yreymihe",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "6xm132vzwi85"
          ],
          "text": "Valley of the Kings"
        },
        {
          "_key": "6t1wy1t3z4b1",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", Karnak, "
        },
        {
          "_key": "4xripfefzohs",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "rj3ophfwj1o9"
          ],
          "text": "Luxor Temple"
        },
        {
          "_key": "042ldakgjy1k",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "_key": "d1nnsn5z6dn0",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "1vjm6crmv0tm"
          ],
          "text": "Abu Simbel"
        },
        {
          "_key": "dh6own1wc1i8",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", Philae, Saqqara, Dahshur, Islamic "
        },
        {
          "_key": "ozn5r3tfwadq",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "fkuw5czt7xz8"
          ],
          "text": "Cairo"
        },
        {
          "_key": "iwgtf6afcvn2",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "_key": "kih9nywwfdgh",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "defdvclnah20"
          ],
          "text": "Coptic Cairo"
        },
        {
          "_key": "qg6woerh5sdt",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ". Lining up names is easy; from that alone, what this country is trying to show doesn't emerge."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "6xm132vzwi85",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.luxor.valley-of-the-kings",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        },
        {
          "_key": "rj3ophfwj1o9",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.luxor.the-luxor-temple",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        },
        {
          "_key": "1vjm6crmv0tm",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-56813",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        },
        {
          "_key": "fkuw5czt7xz8",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-83284",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        },
        {
          "_key": "defdvclnah20",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-63606",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000005",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000004",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Egypt's sites are not scattered attractions. They are kingship, the afterworld, temples, river, desert, and the later Islamic city, layered across long time. Which places you choose ties not only to which era you want to see but to what question you carry about Egypt."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000007",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "i6tgnuo7np1z",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "767ebvt8gxxa"
          ],
          "text": "Giza"
        },
        {
          "_key": "zqz21e2cl7ib",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": " and Saqqara"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "767ebvt8gxxa",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-58854",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000009",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000008",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "For first ancient Egypt, most start at Giza."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000000b",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "jtxilb6zx3vq",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Khufu, Khafre, Menkaure's three pyramids and "
        },
        {
          "_key": "pnbn0mpkbv0y",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "pjdur7y505ca"
          ],
          "text": "the Great Sphinx"
        },
        {
          "_key": "u5zh82q7w6e4",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": " most strongly show Old Kingdom kingship. Here the king doesn't hide. As enormous stone structures, they appear at the border of the Nile Valley and desert. Giza shows how greatly Egypt thought of royal death as a national project."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "pjdur7y505ca",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.giza.the-great-sphinx",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000000d",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000000c",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Giza alone doesn't complete the pyramid story."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000000f",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000000e",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Saqqara has Djoser's Step Pyramid. Here you see the stage where stone monument-architecture is still being invented. Step Pyramid, funerary complex, Old Kingdom officials' mastabas, Unas's Pyramid Texts. If Giza is the scenery of completion, Saqqara is the place of start and development."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000011",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000010",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Adding Dahshur deepens it further. Bent Pyramid and Red Pyramid show trial and error toward the true pyramid. Giza's perfection wasn't born suddenly. After Saqqara and Dahshur, Giza reveals not only stone scale but architectural learning."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000013",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "09n0jq70iqwz",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "fp7juq795255"
          ],
          "text": "Luxor"
        },
        {
          "_key": "4vxq9n1knc74",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": " west bank"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "fp7juq795255",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-58877",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000015",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000014",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "If Giza is \"visible kingship,\" Luxor west bank is \"hidden kingship.\""
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000017",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000016",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "New Kingdom kings, instead of huge pyramids, cut tombs into the Theban west valley. In the Valley of the Kings the centre is corridors and chambers carved into rock, not monuments visible from outside. What matters is walls more than scale. Inside the tombs, the sun god's night journey, the gates of the underworld, and the thought of resurrection are recorded in colour and line."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000019",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000018",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Tutankhamun's tomb is famous; for understanding the Valley of the Kings, it isn't enough. Comparing tombs with different preservation and decoration character — Seti I, Ramesses V/VI, Ramesses III — slowly reads New Kingdom afterworld thought."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000001b",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "h0o0mvznz2su",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "On the west bank, beyond the Valley of the Kings, Hatshepsut's mortuary temple, "
        },
        {
          "_key": "9dcct9ms0xlk",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "kat233gxv55x"
          ],
          "text": "Medinet Habu"
        },
        {
          "_key": "rkwbzxon0dd3",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", and "
        },
        {
          "_key": "mbiy72c1uizf",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "xrbr8lmucpew"
          ],
          "text": "Deir el-Medina"
        },
        {
          "_key": "a358otvdfecn",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": " matter. Deir el-Medina especially is the village of the craftsmen who made the royal tombs. The lives of people who supported royal afterlife make Luxor's understanding considerably human."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "kat233gxv55x",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.luxor.madinat-habu-temple",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        },
        {
          "_key": "xrbr8lmucpew",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.luxor.deir-el-madina",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000001d",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000001c",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Karnak and Luxor Temple"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000001f",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000001e",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "On Luxor east bank, the temple world is central."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000021",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000020",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Karnak isn't one building; it is a huge sanctuary expanded over long time. Pylons, hypostyle hall, obelisks, sacred lake, chapels layer, and several kings' ambitions remain in stone. The temple isn't only a place of prayer. It was where the king showed his relationship to the gods and carved his legitimacy."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000023",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000022",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Luxor Temple is more contained than Karnak, a temple close to the city and river. Visit from evening into night and stone colour layers with town sound — an ancient temple still remaining inside the modern town reads clearly."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000025",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000024",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Better to see these two not as separate sights but as parts of Thebes as a religious city. National scale at Karnak, urban closeness at Luxor Temple. Luxor then stops being \"a town with many ruins\" and becomes a place where religion and kingship shaped the city."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000027",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000026",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Abydos and Dendera"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000029",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000028",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "With margin, a trip north from Luxor is worth considering."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000002b",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000002a",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Abydos is an important sacred place tied to Osiris worship. Seti I's temple, for preservation, relief precision, and quietness of space, is one of Egypt's strongest sites. The king list is famous; not the only thing to come for. Feel the wall carving, light entry, and weight as a pilgrimage place."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000002d",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000002c",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Dendera's Temple of Hathor suits understanding Late and Ptolemaic-Roman temple architecture. Rooftop, crypts, ceiling decoration, multi-columned hall. Egyptian religious architecture continued alive into very late times."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000002f",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000002e",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Abydos and Dendera are often dropped on short first trips. For people who want to see Luxor deeply or who care about temple architecture, very valuable."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000031",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "nzy8aybtytal",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "3sjrd288qu06"
          ],
          "text": "Aswan"
        },
        {
          "_key": "98p0fsxb7rxx",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": " and Nubian temples"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "3sjrd288qu06",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-58758",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000033",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000032",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Aswan plays a slightly different role in Egypt's historical places."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000035",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000034",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "The Nile landform is visible here. Granite islands, the First Cataract, Elephantine, Nubian villages. That Egypt is a country connecting north and south reads as scenery."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000037",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "ecgikqdwkx7o",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Philae is important for Isis worship and at the same time carries the modern heritage-conservation story. The temples at risk of submersion from the "
        },
        {
          "_key": "bjp22l7lfn78",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "16tbbhue3f3h"
          ],
          "text": "Aswan High Dam"
        },
        {
          "_key": "80ld3r9vjqms",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": " were relocated and reassembled on the present island. Visiting, beyond the ancient temple, be aware of how 20th-century conservation changed the landscape."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "16tbbhue3f3h",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-59236",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000039",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000038",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Abu Simbel is a special place farther south from Aswan. Ramesses II's huge rock-cut temples were also political architecture projecting royal power toward Nubia. Far, but the distance itself has meaning. Border, empire, and royal self-expression concentrate on one rock façade."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000003b",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000003a",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "The era after Cairo"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000003d",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000003c",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "End Egypt's historical places at antiquity and you miss more than half the country."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000003f",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "xh2gys23xto8",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Islamic Cairo is a city where Fatimid, Ayyubid, Mamluk, and Ottoman architecture layer. Al-Muizz Street, "
        },
        {
          "_key": "c9h1gho1tx63",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "f1q8mynr37np"
          ],
          "text": "Bab Zuweila"
        },
        {
          "_key": "umprmkqm0vom",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", Sultan Hasan Mosque, Ibn Tulun Mosque, around al-Azhar. Stone monuments here aren't royal tombs but for faith, scholarship, commerce, and urban life."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "f1q8mynr37np",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.cairo.bab-zuweila",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000041",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "988wxxw0711n",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Coptic Cairo matters too. Around "
        },
        {
          "_key": "xse9sdqm2smw",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "zetf6n23u4bg"
          ],
          "text": "the Hanging Church"
        },
        {
          "_key": "kaonej4knez1",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", the Church of Saints Sergius and Bacchus, and the "
        },
        {
          "_key": "l3y5kgbnz78n",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "m6ppn86rcek0"
          ],
          "text": "Ben Ezra Synagogue"
        },
        {
          "_key": "yt0dx53oetia",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": " is a place for thinking about Egypt's Christian and Jewish histories. Antiquity, Christianity, and Islam layering in the same city makes Cairo not just a gateway but a thick historical destination."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "zetf6n23u4bg",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.cairo.the-hanging-church",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        },
        {
          "_key": "m6ppn86rcek0",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.cairo.ben-ezra-synagogue",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000043",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000042",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "The Grand Egyptian Museum has also changed the way to see Giza and Cairo. Near the pyramids, you can see ancient Egyptian objects in a large context, so Giza is no longer \"the place to end at morning pyramids.\" Seeing plateau and museum on the same day or split over two days makes building and object, tomb and grave goods, easier to connect."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000045",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000044",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "What peripheral places teach"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000047",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000046",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Famous sites alone don't teach Egypt."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000049",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000048",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "In Fayoum, places like Hawara, Lahun, and Karanis show pyramid, Middle Kingdom, and Greco-Roman life from another angle. Few visitors, varying preservation; Egypt isn't only royal capitals and temples."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000004b",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000004a",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "In Kharga Oasis, Hibis Temple and the el-Bagawat cemetery show how ancient religion and early Christianity remained inside the desert. Western Desert sites aren't easy to move through. A different sense of time from the centre."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000004d",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000004c",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Aswan's Elephantine Island and Luxor's Deir el-Medina hold value different from huge monuments. Beyond king and god, traces of border guardians, workers, and inhabitants."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000004f",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000004e",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "How to choose"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000051",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000050",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "For first trips, Cairo, Giza, Luxor, and Aswan as the axis is natural. Old Kingdom, New Kingdom, the Nile, and the southern landscape appear."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000053",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000052",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "For deeper sites, adding Saqqara, Dahshur, Dendera, Abydos, and Deir el-Medina widens Egypt's understanding greatly. With limited time, staying long in one region beats adding count."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000055",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000054",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "For religious city interest, take time in Islamic Cairo and Coptic Cairo. Egypt is not only the pharaoh's country but also a country with long Christian and Islamic histories."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000057",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000056",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "For quiet and peripheral places, consider Fayoum, the Western Desert, and oases. Confirm movement, permits, guide, and local conditions. Don't build with the sense of standard city sightseeing."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000059",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000058",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Egypt's historical places aren't to be seen all."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000005b",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000005a",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Choose the reading order to match your own question."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000005d",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000005c",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "The pyramids speak of kingship. The Valley of the Kings speaks of the afterworld. Karnak speaks of the temple state. Aswan speaks of the river and the southern border. Cairo speaks of Egypt continued after antiquity."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000005f",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000005e",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "See that connection and Egypt stops being a collection of sights. It looks like one long civilisation continuing to speak across changing places."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    }
  ],
